    <statement><p>Find the linear approximation to <m>f(x,y,z)=\frac{xy}{z}</m> at the point <m>(1,-3,-1)</m>:</p>
<p><m>f(x,y,z)\approx</m><fillin characters="20" name="AnSwEr0001"/></p>
</statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>3x-y+3z</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <solution><m>(1,-3,-1)</m>:

<me>\left. \begin{array}{lcl}
f(x,y,z)=\frac{xy}{z} \amp \amp  f(1,-3,-1)=3 \\
f_x(x,y,z)=\frac{y}{z} \amp  \Rightarrow \amp  f_x(1,-3,-1)=3 \\
f_y(x,y,z)=\frac{x}{z}  \amp \amp  f_y(1,-3,-1)=-1 \\
f_z(x,y,z)=-\frac{xy}{z^{2}} \amp \amp  f_z(1,-3,-1)=3
\end{array} \right.</me>

The linear approximation is therefore
<me>f(x,y,z)\approx f(1,-3,-1)+f_x(1,-3,-1)(x-1)+</me><me>f_y(1,-3,-1)(y + 3)+f_z(1,-3,-1)(z + 1)</me>
<me>f(x,y,z)\approx 3+3\mathopen{}\left(x-1\right)-\left(y+3\right)+3\mathopen{}\left(z+1\right)</me>
<me>f(x,y,z)\approx 3x-y+3z</me>





</solution>
